WEBVTT

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:04.720
All right, here's a short demo showing Superlogical working with remote hosts.

00:00:05.240 --> 00:00:08.480
This is going to be a short demo, not going to dive into details of everything,

00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:11.800
not going to explain how everything works, but I hope you enjoy it.

00:00:11.820 --> 00:00:14.680
I hope you get an idea about how this stuff is going to look.

00:00:15.160 --> 00:00:19.420
So first, when I start up, I'm just in my local application, local session.

00:00:19.900 --> 00:00:23.040
Of course, even though it's local, it's persistent, so I could type,

00:00:23.320 --> 00:00:25.260
and I could reopen the application, and that's saved.

00:00:26.020 --> 00:00:27.580
But I could also connect to remote hosts.

00:00:27.580 --> 00:00:30.660
We don't just want persistent sessions locally, we want them remotely.

00:00:30.980 --> 00:00:35.320
So let me open the command palette, add a remote host, and I have some infrastructure here.

00:00:35.800 --> 00:00:40.580
This is actually a VPS that's running remotely that's connected to my tailnet,

00:00:40.820 --> 00:00:43.400
so I could connect to that, and we're in.

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:46.320
And, you know, it looks like an SSH session.

00:00:46.940 --> 00:00:51.320
But I could open a split, and I have two now, and this is over the same connection.

00:00:51.860 --> 00:00:55.200
And I also want to highlight that this is a real login.

00:00:55.200 --> 00:00:58.760
So if I list my logins, this is a real thing.

00:00:59.500 --> 00:01:00.440
Rex is the multiplexer name.

00:01:00.720 --> 00:01:03.800
So this isn't just a terminal opening via our server.

00:01:04.100 --> 00:01:07.960
Our server does a full SSH-style system login.

00:01:08.300 --> 00:01:15.340
So that shows up who works, all of your login shell stuff is loaded,

00:01:15.540 --> 00:01:22.200
all of your per-user limits are honored and respected on your Linux machine, etc., etc., etc.

00:01:22.200 --> 00:01:25.880
This is a full login, and actually Rex is a full SSH replacement,

00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:28.160
but I'm not going to talk too much about that here.

00:01:28.940 --> 00:01:31.160
I actually don't have SSH on this machine at all.

00:01:31.540 --> 00:01:32.520
This is how I get in.

00:01:32.700 --> 00:01:34.800
But, yeah, so let's create a split.

00:01:35.060 --> 00:01:35.600
Let's start there.

00:01:36.020 --> 00:01:40.420
Next thing, if you look up here, the machine showed up in our session list,

00:01:40.660 --> 00:01:43.680
and we got the new session, and every new session gets a unique name.

00:01:43.740 --> 00:01:44.600
This is Drifting Cedar.

00:01:45.140 --> 00:01:46.300
We could just rename it.

00:01:46.400 --> 00:01:48.840
So I could rename this session, and then say it's the demo session.

00:01:49.360 --> 00:01:50.280
And there you go, it's the demo.

00:01:50.960 --> 00:01:52.700
Let's go hop back to our Mac.

00:01:52.960 --> 00:01:55.780
So we're back on the Mac, and I want to actually go to the CLI.

00:01:55.880 --> 00:01:58.500
So every terminal session gets this injected.

00:01:58.660 --> 00:02:00.700
So on my local machine, I have it.

00:02:00.760 --> 00:02:03.580
If I go back to this one, it's also installed here.

00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:06.500
But let's go back up here.

00:02:06.900 --> 00:02:10.260
And the first thing I want to do is actually not this.

00:02:10.260 --> 00:02:12.640
I want to do what is it called, like whoami?

00:02:13.280 --> 00:02:15.180
The whoami tells you who you're logged in as.

00:02:15.180 --> 00:02:21.860
So I'm connecting, I'm on my Mac, connecting to Hill, and it's telling me who I am and how I got there.

00:02:22.040 --> 00:02:24.840
So I connected as MitchellH at GitHub.

00:02:25.180 --> 00:02:26.560
That's my Tailscale identity.

00:02:26.560 --> 00:02:28.780
Since I connected over Tailscale, that's how I got my identity.

00:02:29.220 --> 00:02:34.880
Over Tailscale via a couple hops from this machine, and I'm acting as MitchellH.

00:02:35.220 --> 00:02:40.300
I did hint earlier that this is a full SSH replacement, so I could also log in as root.

00:02:40.900 --> 00:02:41.420
And that's root.

00:02:41.420 --> 00:02:49.260
Like I said, I'm not going to get into the full details of the SSH replacement thing, but there is a mapping on the server side of who can act as who and how they authenticate.

00:02:49.480 --> 00:02:52.320
And we respect preexisting SSH keys and everything.

00:02:52.440 --> 00:02:54.020
So this is all just working.

00:02:54.920 --> 00:02:56.440
So I could log in as both.

00:02:57.040 --> 00:02:59.700
But we could do some other fun stuff with the CLI that I want to show you.

00:02:59.840 --> 00:03:03.020
So if we look, we have the demo session, right?

00:03:03.020 --> 00:03:08.400
Okay, but here on my Mac, I could create a new session, and Crisp Sierra has showed up.

00:03:09.060 --> 00:03:11.440
If I go there, you could see that it's empty.

00:03:11.520 --> 00:03:11.840
It's new.

00:03:11.980 --> 00:03:12.780
I could open a tab.

00:03:13.480 --> 00:03:18.040
But it's clearly different from the demo where, you know, I dropped this up.

00:03:18.100 --> 00:03:20.840
Let's just put some things in here so we could see, and let's go root.

00:03:22.260 --> 00:03:27.160
The other thing I want you to notice is as I'm typing, it's hard to tell, of course, over video, but as I'm typing, this is really responsive.

00:03:27.160 --> 00:03:34.840
This is a remote server that's actually across the country for me, and it feels local.

00:03:35.640 --> 00:03:43.780
We bring in a lot of the architectural similarities as Mosh into this, and so it's going to continue to get better.

00:03:43.860 --> 00:03:47.220
We don't do everything Mosh does, but I get asked a lot if we do some stuff, and we do.

00:03:47.600 --> 00:03:55.040
So I'm not ready to go into details exactly of the full protocol and transport, but it's super responsive.

00:03:55.040 --> 00:04:05.260
The other thing I want to notice is if I quit, quit my local app, it'll be a little slower to load this time, but we reconnect, and we go back into the last session we were in.

00:04:05.440 --> 00:04:07.540
So I immediately went in, and we're still here.

00:04:08.120 --> 00:04:10.120
And, of course, all our sessions are still here.

00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:21.020
Next, if I grab this ID here, I could actually go ahead and do a session kill, and I want you to notice how fast this is.

00:04:21.080 --> 00:04:22.000
So we have this here.

00:04:22.320 --> 00:04:23.380
I'm talking to this server.

00:04:23.380 --> 00:04:26.520
If I hit go and reopen, it's gone already.

00:04:26.960 --> 00:04:28.600
All this stuff is super fast.

00:04:28.700 --> 00:04:30.640
It propagates to all the clients really quickly.

00:04:31.580 --> 00:04:37.340
And, again, this is all about bringing down the barrier so that everything feels local.

00:04:37.880 --> 00:04:42.780
The barrier between local and remote has to disappear.

00:04:43.000 --> 00:04:47.820
It all has to feel like it's right at your fingertips, and that's built in all the way through.

00:04:49.100 --> 00:04:51.340
Okay, I want to show one other kind of fun feature.

00:04:51.340 --> 00:04:56.680
So if I go over here, we added a feature where if you do Command-Shift-G, we could jump.

00:04:56.860 --> 00:04:58.100
It's a Go to Directory feature.

00:04:58.100 --> 00:05:03.560
So we could go ahead and jump, let's say, to the macOS folder, and it opens a new terminal in that folder.

00:05:03.700 --> 00:05:04.680
So you could see that we're here.

00:05:05.660 --> 00:05:06.740
Okay, makes sense, right?

00:05:07.180 --> 00:05:13.700
Well, the cool part is if we go ahead and go over here, I'm now on my remote machine in the root directory, and I do that again.

00:05:14.060 --> 00:05:14.780
It works.

00:05:15.320 --> 00:05:18.000
Go to Directory works over remote connections as well.

00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:19.000
So I could go to proc.

00:05:19.080 --> 00:05:20.400
It lists the things in proc.

00:05:20.620 --> 00:05:21.580
I could go to home.

00:05:21.680 --> 00:05:22.620
We could see some users.

00:05:22.780 --> 00:05:25.580
I could go back to Mitchell H, hit enter, and there we go.

00:05:25.980 --> 00:05:26.660
We're in there.

00:05:27.660 --> 00:05:32.920
Again, this is all about dropping the boundary of local versus remote.

00:05:33.060 --> 00:05:34.920
Everything that works local should work remote.

00:05:35.020 --> 00:05:36.520
Everything that works remote should work local.

00:05:36.520 --> 00:05:42.400
And that permeates through everything in our design here and functionality.

00:05:43.880 --> 00:05:48.380
The way that Go to Directory works over remote connections is really cool.

00:05:48.980 --> 00:05:54.280
We're going to have to wait for future details of how our architecture works to enable things like this.

00:05:54.680 --> 00:05:58.380
And we'll continue to show more functionality as we get closer.

00:05:58.600 --> 00:06:01.960
But that's a demo of remote hosts.

00:06:01.960 --> 00:06:07.040
Some hints towards some really cool stuff like Rex being an SSH replacement.

00:06:07.740 --> 00:06:12.000
Some sneaky little protocol things in there for both performance and functionality.

00:06:12.820 --> 00:06:13.660
There's a lot here.

00:06:13.820 --> 00:06:16.140
So hope you liked that demo and talk to you soon.
